"(PDF) Structure-based identification of a novel NTPase from (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/12895555_Structure-based_identification_of_a_novel_NTPase_from)\nciteturn530view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn529view0\",\"lineno\":360}); Total lines: 1741\nL140: functionally unknown hypothetical proteins. We present a structure-based identification of the biochemical function\nL141: \nL142: of a protein with an as yet unknown function from a M. jannaschii gene, Mj0226. The crystal structure of Mj0226\nL143: \nL144: protein determined at 2.2 Å resolution reveals that the protein is a homodimer and each monomer folds into an\nL145: \nL146: elongated a/b structure of a new fold family. Comparisons of Mj0226 protein with protein structures in the database,\nL147: however, indicate that one part of the protein is homologous to some of the nucleotide-binding proteins. Biochemical\nL148: \nL149: analysis shows that Mj0226 protein is a novel nucleotide triphosphatase that can efficiently hydrolyze nonstandard\nL150: \nL151: nucleotides such as XTP to XMP or ITP to IMP, but not the standard nucleotides, in the presence of Mg 2+ or Mn 2+ ions.\nL152: \nL153: 1\nL154: \nL155: These authors contributed equally to this work.\nL156: \nL157: 2\nL158: Structural Biology Center, Korea Institute of Science and Technology, P.O. Box 131, Cheongryang, Seoul 130-650,\nL159: \nL160: South Korea.\nL161: \nL162: 3\nL163: \nL164: Graduate School of Biotechnology, Korea University, Seoul, South Korea.\nL165: \nL166: 4\nL167: \nL168: Department of Chemistry and E.O. Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory,\nL169: \nL170: University of California, Berkeley, California 94720, USA.\nL171: \nL172: Correspondence should be addressed to Y.C. email: yunje@sbc4.kist.re.kr\nL173: Fig. 1 a,A stereodiagram of the experimental (MIR) electron density map at 3.0 Å resolution. The map is contoured at 1 s, with the final model dis-\nL174: \nL175: played for comparison. b, Overall structure of Mj0226 protein dimer. The helices are colored blue, b-strands are violet and loops are green. The N-\nL176: \nL177: and C-terminus of the protein are labeled, and the helices and strands in one monomer are numbered. The secondary structure assignment was\nL178: based on the program Procheck 42. c, Overall structure of Mj0226 protein dimer rotated by 90° from the view in (b). d, A diagram showing the sol-\nL179: \nL180: vent-accessible surface (GRASP 43) of Mj0226 protein dimer. One monomer is colored blue and the other violet, and the AMPPNP is shown in yellow.\nL181: \nL182: Some of the conserved residues are labeled.\nL183: \nL184: a b\nL185: \nL186: c d\nL187: \nL188: © 1999 Nature America Inc. • http://structbio.nature.com\nL189: \nL190: © 1999 Nature America Inc. • http://structbio.nature.com\nL191: \nL192: articles\nL193: fraction of genes coding for hypothetical proteins with no known\nL194: \nL195: functions\nL196: \nL197: 1,2,6–12\nL198: \nL199: . With recent advances in technology, software,\nL200: \nL201: and methods for three-dimensional structure determination, the\nL202: \nL203: structure-based prediction of molecular functions emerges as a\nL204: \nL205: viable method for ‘hypothetical proteins’\nL206: \nL207: 13–16\nL208: \nL209: , the proteins with\nL210: \nL211: no sequence homologies to those with known functions.\nL212: \nL213: Biochemical or biophysical (molecular) function of a protein is\nL214: tightly correlated to its three-dimensional structure, which is pri-\nL215: \nL216: marily governed by its amino acid sequence. However, it is often\nL217: \nL218: observed that proteins with low sequence identities can fold into\nL219: \nL220: similar structures and have related functions\nL221: \nL222: 17–19\nL223: \nL224: . Furthermore, it\nL225: \nL226: has been shown recently that proteins with high sequence similar-\nL227: \nL228: ity (up to 50% sequence identity) could form completely different\nL229: \nL230: structures\nL231: \nL232: 20\nL233: \nL234: . Thus, the structural information of a hypothetical\nL235: protein may extend the limitations of sequence-based prediction\nL236: \nL237: of molecular function and play an impor-\nL238: \nL239: tant role in predicting its molecular func-\nL240: \nL241: tion. Here, we provide an example in\nL242: \nL243: which the structure determination fol-\nL244: \nL245: lowed by biochemical assays can elucidate\nL246: \nL247: a possible molecular function of a hypo-\nL248: \nL249: thetical protein.\nL250: \nL251: Overall structure\nL252: \nL253: The structure of a protein coded by a hypo-\nL254: \nL255: thetical gene of M. jannaschii, Mj0226, has\nL256: \nL257: been determined by the multiple isomor-\nL258: phous replacement (MIR) method and\nL259: \nL260: refined to a crystallographic R-factor of\nL261: \nL262: 18.9% at 2.2 Å resolution (Fig. 1 a; Table 1).\nL263: \nL264: Both gel filtration and dynamic light-scat-\nL265: \nL266: tering studies show that Mj0226 protein\nL267: \nL268: forms a dimer under physiological condi-\nL269: \nL270: tions (data not shown). Consistent with\nL271: \nL272: our biochemical analysis, the crystal struc-\nL273: \nL274: ture of Mj0226 protein reveals that the pro-\nL275: \nL276: tein is a homodimer. The monomer\nL277: \nL278: structure has an a/b fold that has not been\nL279: \nL280: previously observed in other structures.\nL281: The overall structure has an elongated\nL282: \nL283: shape consisting of two lobes connected by\nL284: \nL285: 692 nature structural biology • volume 6 number 6 • june 1999\nL286: \nL287: a b-sheet, and has dimensions of 27 Å ´34 Å ´50 Å (Fig. 1 b,c).\nL288: \nL289: Three long antiparallel b-strands (b3, b4 and b5) form a twisted\nL290: \nL291: b-sheet composed at the center of a monomer. In one lobe, one\nL292: \nL293: face of the central b-sheet is surrounded by several loops and\nL294: \nL295: helices (a 3–a 7), whereas the other face is completely exposed to\nL296: solvent. The strand b 3 in this lobe joins a short strand b 4 in an\nL297: \nL298: antiparallel manner. In the other lobe, b 3 is joined by two short N-\nL299: \nL300: terminal b-strands (b 1 and b 2) to form a five-stranded b-sheet,\nL301: \nL302: and this sheet is surrounded by three a-helices (a 1, a 2 and a 8). A\nL303: \nL304: cleft 9 Å wide, 28 Å long and 11 Å deep is formed between the two\nL305: \nL306: lobes (Fig. 1 d). Two monomers of Mj0226 are related by a non-\nL307: \nL308: crystallographic two-fold axis that is parallel to the length of the\nL309: central b-sheet. The dimeric interface is formed by the packing of\nL310: \nL311: portions of the helices a 2, a 3, a 4 and a loop between b 2 and a 2,\nL312: \nL313: and has a buried surface area of 1,887 Å\nL314: \nL315: 2\nL316: \nL317: . The interactions between\nL318: \nL319: the two monomers are primarily hydrophobic in nature (77% of\nL320: \nL321: the buried area is nonpolar). Several hydrophobic clusters are\nL322: \nL323: formed by the side chains of 10 nonpolar residues (Fig. 2). The\nL324: \nL325: first hydrophobic cluster includes Leu 81 and Phe 84 from strand\nL326: b 4, and Phe 91, Val 92 and Tyr 88 from helix a 3. The second clus-\nL327: \nL328: ter consists of Pro 44 and Ile 46 from the loop between b 2 and a 2,\nL329: \nL330: and Phe 56 and Trp 60 from helix a 2. The side chain of Leu 104\nL331: \nL332: from helix a 4 in each monomer interact with one another. In\nL333: \nL334: addition, some intermonomer backbone–backbone interactions\nL335: \nL336: are formed in the loop region between b 2 and a 2. We also\nL337: \nL338: observed an intermonomer salt link between Lys 103 and Glu 106.\nL339: \nL340: Structure-based functional analyses\nL341: Since the crystal structure did not contain any bound ligand to\nL342: \nL343: help infer the protein’s possible biochemical function\nL344: \nL345: 21\nL346: \nL347: , we\nL348: \nL349: searched for its structural homologs in the Protein Data Bank\nL350: \nL351: with the programs DALI\nL352: \nL353: 22\nL354: \nL355: and SHEBA (J.S. Kim & B.K. Lee,\nL356: \nL357: unpublished program). Although the structure has a new fold,\nL358: \nL359: the search revealed a good structural similarity between one lobe\nL360: \nL361: of the structure and the C-terminal putative anticodon-binding\nL362: \nL363: domain of Thermus thermophilus histidyl-tRNA synthetase\nL364: \nL365: 23\nL366: (1adj, z score = 5.9) or glycyl-tRNA synthetase\nL367: \nL368: 24\nL369: \nL370: (1ati, z\nL371: \nL372: Fig. 2 A diagram showing\nL373: \nL374: the dimeric interface of\nL375: \nL376: Mj0226. Several nonpolar\nL377: \nL378: residues are involved in the\nL379: \nL380: formation of hydrophobic\nL381: \nL382: clusters as explained in the\nL383: \nL384: text.\nL385: \nL386: Table 1 Statistics of crystallographic data for Mj0226\nL387: \nL388: Native Thimerosal KAu(CN)\nL389: \nL390: 4\nL391: \nL392: PCMB\nL393: \nL394: KAuCl\nL395: \nL396: 4\nL397: \nL398: Data collection\nL399: \nL400: Resolution (Å) 2.2 2.8 2.8 2.8 2.8\nL401: \nL402: Unique reflections 23,733 10,711 10,158 11,225 11,465\nL403: \nL404: Completeness (%) 98.5 90.3 85.9 94.1 97.8\nL405: \nL406: R\nL407: \nL408: sym\nL409: \nL410: (%)\nL411: \nL412: 1\nL413: 5.2 9.4 6.6 8.9 7.0\nL414: \nL415: R\nL416: \nL417: iso\nL418: \nL419: (%) – 16.3 12.5 11.0 11.1\nL420: \nL421: Phasing power\nL422: \nL423: Centric – 1.22 0.77 0.87 0.59\nL424: \nL425: Acentric – 1.58 1.00 1.11 0.85\nL426: \nL427: Refinement statistics\nL428: \nL429: Mj0226 Mj0226–AMPPNP\nL430: \nL431: Resolution (Å) 6.0–2.2 8.0–2.2\nL432: \nL433: Reflections 20,346 21,998\nL434: \nL435: R\nL436: \nL437: cryst\nL438: \nL439: (%) 18.9 20.0\nL440: \nL441: R\nL442: \nL443: free\nL444: \nL445: (%) 26.7 25.3\nL446: \nL447: R.m.s. deviations\nL448: \nL449: 2\nL450: \nL451: Bond lengths (Å) 0.011 0.012\nL452: \nL453: Angles (°) 1.5 1.6\nL454: \nL455: 1\nL456: \nL457: R\nL458: \nL459: sym\nL460: \nL461: = S\nL462: \nL463: h\nL464: