res in the range of 2.2–3.1\nL549: \nL550: and have r.m.s. differences of 2.5–4.0 Å for 62–95 paired C a\nL551: \nL552: atoms. These proteins include CheY (1tmy)\nL553: \nL554: 25\nL555: \nL556: , deoxynucleoside\nL557: \nL558: monophosphate kinase (1dek)\nL559: \nL560: 26\nL561: \nL562: , integrin fragment (1ido)\nL563: \nL564: 27\nL565: \nL566: ,\nL567: porphobilinogen deaminase (1pda)\nL568: \nL569: 28\nL570: \nL571: , glycine amidoribo-\nL572: \nL573: nucleotide (GAR) transformylase (1gar)\nL574: \nL575: 29\nL576: \nL577: , a tryptic domain of\nL578: \nL579: lactose repressor (1tlf)\nL580: \nL581: 30\nL582: \nL583: , nitrite response regulator (1rnl)\nL584: \nL585: 31\nL586: \nL587: and\nL588: \nL589: flavodoxin (1rcf)\nL590: \nL591: 32\nL592: \nL593: . When each of these structures was super-\nL594: \nL595: imposed onto the Mj0226 monomer, GAR transformylase\nL596: \nL597: 29\nL598: \nL599: (z\nL600: \nL601: nature structural biology • volume 6 number 6 • june 1999 693\nL602: \nL603: score = 2.6) and flavodoxin\nL604: \nL605: 32\nL606: \nL607: (z score =\nL608: \nL609: 2.4) show relatively higher similarity to\nL610: Mj0226 in overall topology as compared\nL611: \nL612: to the others (Fig. 3 b). Each of these pro-\nL613: \nL614: teins has a common feature of a mixed b-\nL615: \nL616: sheet flanked by helices. Therefore, we\nL617: \nL618: examined whether the Mj0226 protein\nL619: \nL620: showed GAR transformylase activity or\nL621: \nL622: flavin mononucleotide (FMN) binding,\nL623: \nL624: but neither of these activities was observed\nL625: \nL626: (data not shown).\nL627: \nL628: At this stage, we noticed that most of the structural homologs\nL629: \nL630: we identified had one common property, that is, they bound\nL631: some form of nucleotide. Therefore, we examined the nucleotide\nL632: \nL633: binding properties of Mj0226. Initial nucleotide binding assays\nL634: \nL635: on Mj0226 protein using GTP and ATP (because of their avail-\nL636: \nL637: ability) showed that the protein interacts with both nucleotides.\nL638: \nL639: It has also been observed that a noticeable amount of GMP and\nL640: \nL641: much smaller amount of AMP were formed by the hydrolysis of\nL642: \nL643: GTP and ATP, respectively (Fig. 4 a). Thus, we have analyzed\nL644: \nL645: quantitatively the nucleotide triphosphatase (NTPase) activity of\nL646: Mj0226 for all available NTPs. Xanthine triphosphate (XTP) is\nL647: \nL648: so far the best substrate for Mj0226 protein, followed by ITP,\nL649: \nL650: GTP or dGTP, both of which are hydrolyzed ~100-fold less effi-\nL651: \nL652: ciently than XTP, and, finally, ATP, CTP, and TTP, which are\nL653: \nL654: hydrolyzed the least well (Fig. 4 b; Table 2). XDP or GDP did not\nL655: \nL656: hydrolyze to XMP or GMP, suggesting that Mj0226 is a\nL657: \nL658: pyrophosphate-releasing NTPase (Fig. 4 c). The NTPase activities\nL659: \nL660: are most efficient in the presence of either Mg\nL661: \nL662: 2+\nL663: \nL664: or Mn\nL665: \nL666: 2+\nL667: at 80 °C,\nL668: \nL669: which is close to the optimum growth temperature of\nL670: \nL671: M. jannaschii. In the absence of these metal ions, Mj0226 protein\nL672: \nL673: shows ~100-fold decrease in activity (Fig. 4 b).\nL674: \nL675: Nucleotide binding site\nL676: \nL677: In an attempt to predict the nucleotide binding site of Mj0226\nL678: \nL679: protein, we searched for the conserved residues among several\nL680: \nL681: Fig. 3 a,Structure comparison of Mj0226 pro-\nL682: \nL683: tein (red) with the C-terminal domains of his-\nL684: \nL685: tidyl- (blue) and glycyl- (cyan) tRNA\nL686: synthetases. b,Structure comparison of Mj0226\nL687: \nL688: protein (red) with flavodoxin (blue). The ligand\nL689: \nL690: (AMPPNP or FMN) for each protein is shown in\nL691: \nL692: thick red. c, Comparison of topologies in four\nL693: \nL694: different proteins, Mj0226, histidyl-tRNA syn-\nL695: \nL696: thetase domain, flavodoxin and GAR trans-\nL697: \nL698: formylase.\nL699: \nL700: a b\nL701: \nL702: c\nL703: \nL704: © 1999 Nature America Inc. • http://structbio.nature.com\nL705: \nL706: © 1999 Nature America Inc. • http://structbio.nature.com\nL707: \nL708: articles\nL709: \nL710: homologous hypothetical proteins found in other organisms from\nL711: all of the three superkingdoms of life (Fig. 5) Sequence alignment of\nL712: \nL713: these proteins reveals several conserved residues, and a majority of\nL714: \nL715: these residues are clustered in the cleft between the two lobes of the\nL716: \nL717: protein, implying that the nucleotide binding site is likely to be in\nL718: \nL719: this area (Fig. 5).\nL720: \nL721: To find the nucleotide binding site, the substrate XTP, ITP or\nL722: \nL723: GTP, or the product XMP or IMP was soaked into the stabilizing\nL724: \nL725: buffer containing Mj0226 protein crystals. However, the crystals\nL726: were severely damaged upon addition of the substrate or product,\nL727: \nL728: implying conformational changes in the protein.\nL729: \nL730: Thus, we have added AMPPNP, an analog of the less efficient sub-\nL731: \nL732: strate ATP, into the stabilizing buffer containing the crystals. The\nL733: \nL734: resulting difference map shows the presence of AMPPNP within the\nL735: \nL736: cleft between the two lobes (Fig. 6 a). However, we observed a clear\nL737: \nL738: density of the nucleotide analog only in one monomer.\nL739: \nL740: Furthermore, the observation that the electron density of the base\nL741: moiety of AMPPNP is relatively weak compared with the rest of the\nL742: \nL743: nucleotide is consistent with ATP being a poor substrate.\nL744: \nL745: The binding site and the mode of binding of AMPPNP are dif-\nL746: \nL747: 694 nature structural biology • volume 6 number 6 • june 1999\nL748: \nL749: ferent from those found in the three proteins that have limited\nL750: \nL751: structural homologies to Mj0266 protein mentioned earlier (Fig\nL752: \nL753: 3 b). The oxygen atoms in the phosphate groups of AMPPNP\nL754: \nL755: form several hydrogen bonds with the side chain atoms of Ser 74\nL756: (2.9 Å, 3.2 Å), Ser 89 (3.3 Å), Asp 152 (3.2 Å, O d 1 atom is likely\nL757: \nL758: to be protonated), His 177 (2.8 Å), Arg 178 (2.7 Å), and the main\nL759: \nL760: chain amides of Ser 74 (3.4 Å) and Gly 75 (2.6 Å) (Fig. 6b). The\nL761: \nL762: O3 atom of the sugar ring makes hydrogen bonds to the side\nL763: \nL764: chains of Arg 178 (2.6 Å) and Glu 23 (2.6 Å). The base moiety\nL765: \nL766: forms a hydrophobic interaction with the side chain of Phe 149,\nL767: \nL768: and the N6 atom of the base hydrogen bonds to the side chain of\nL769: Asn 112 (2.9 Å) from a symmetry-related molecule. When the\nL770: \nL771: xanthine is modeled to replace the adenine base of AMPPNP, the\nL772: \nL773: O2 atom of XTP, which is absent in adenine, would form a\nL774: \nL775: hydrogen bond with N d 2 of Asn 17, a strictly conserved residue,\nL776: \nL777: whose O d 1 group is hydrogen bonded to the backbone amide\nL778: \nL779: group of Asn 19. While the O2 atom of XTP is not present in ino-\nL780: \nL781: sine, the amino group is in the equivalent position of guanine.\nL782: \nL783: This may explain why GTP is a less efficient substrate compared\nL784: to XTP or ITP. Also the O6 atom of xanthine, inosine or guanine\nL785: \nL786: could form a hydrogen bond to the N d 2 of Asn 19 with slight\nL787: \nL788: conformational changes. However, it is possible that the binding\nL789: \nL790: of a substrate may induce local conformational changes in the\nL791: \nL792: active site, consistent with the observation that crystals were\nL793: \nL794: damaged upon addition of substrate or product.\nL795: \nL796: Implication of biological function\nL797: \nL798: Since no XTPase has been identified from any organism, the cellu-\nL799: lar role of Mj0226 protein is difficult to predict. However, the pos-\nL800: \nL801: sible biochemical function of the enzyme deduced from our\nL802: \nL803: structural studies provides clues for understanding its cellular role.\nL804: \nL805: Fig 4 a,Thin layer chromatography showing the pyrophosphate releasing\nL806: \nL807: NTPase activities of Mj0226 with GTP. Lane 1: control GTP; 2: GTP with\nL808: \nL809: Mj0226 (0.3 nM); 3: GTP with Mj0226 (3 nM); 4: GTP with Mj0226 (30 nM); 5:\nL810: \nL811: GTP with Mj0226 (30 nM) and EDTA (10 mM); 6: GTP with BSA (10 m g).\nL812: b,HPLC chromatograms showing XTPase activities of Mj0226. Top: XTP in\nL813: \nL814: the absence of Mj0226; middle: XTP incubated with Mj0226 and 5 mM\nL815: \nL816: MgCl 2; bottom: XTP incubated with only Mj0226. c,HPLC chromatograms\nL817: \nL818: showing XDPase activities of Mj0226. Top: XDP in the absence of Mj0226:\nL819: \nL820: bottom: XDP incubated with Mj0226 and 5 mM MgCl 2.\nL821: \nL822: a b\nL823: \nL824: c\nL825: \nL826: Table 2 Kinetic parameters of Mj0226 with various nucleotides\nL827: \nL828: K\nL829: \nL830: cat\nL831: \nL832: (s\nL833: \nL834: -1\nL835: \nL836: )K\nL837: \nL838: m\nL839: \nL840: (mM) K\nL841: \nL842: cat\nL843: \nL844: / K\nL845: \nL846: m\nL847: \nL848: XTP 1009.37 0.10 10195.66\nL849: \nL850: ITP 911.72 0.15 5998.16\nL851: GTP 97.65 1.11 87.66\nL852: \nL853: dGTP 96.64 1.13 85.52\nL854: \nL855: ATP 1.02 7.04 0.15\nL856: \nL857: CTP 2.23 1.45 1.54\nL858: \nL859: TTP 1.77 0.30 5.90\nL860: \nL861: © 1999 Nature America Inc. • http://structbio.nature.com\nL862: \nL863: © 1999 Nature America Inc. • http://structbio.nature.com\nL864: \nL865: articles\nL866: \nL867: Recently, it has been observed in Saccharomyces cerevisiae\nL868: \nL869: that inactivation of a gene (gene I.D. P47119) resulted in\nL870: \nL871: increased sensitivity of yeast to the base analog 6-N-hydroxy-\nL872: \nL873: aminopurine (HAP). In addition, adenine-requiring haploid\nL874: strains are unable to grow on HAP as the sole adenine source,\nL875: \nL876: presumably because the conversion of HAP to HAP triphosphate\nL877: \nL878: and subsequent incorporation into DNA is lethal\nL879: \nL880: 33–36\nL881: \nL882: . This yeast\nL883: \nL884: protein is homologous (about 30 % sequence identity) to Mj0226\nL885: \nL886: protein (Fig. 5). Our structural and biochemical information, in\nL887: \nL888: conjunction with the moderate sequence similarity, suggest that\nL889: \nL890: nature structural biology • volume 6 number 6 • june 1999 695\nL891: the cellular function of Mj0226 protein may be to prevent muta-\nL892: \nL893: tions by protecting DNA from the incorporation of modified\nL894: \nL895: purine bases such as dXTP or dITP. Alternatively, the yeast pro-\nL896: \nL897: tein may be a HAPTP/dHAPTP triphosphatase.\nL898: \nL899: Although we have not tested the NTPase activities of Mj0226\nL900: \nL901: protein on dITP and dXTP (not available commercially), both\nL902: \nL903: substrates are likely to be hydrolyzed to a similar degree as ITP\nL904: \nL905: and XTP, respectively, since there is no strong interaction at the 2'\nL906: oxygen of the sugar ring in the structure, and both dGTP and\nL907: \nL908: GTP show similar kinetic parameters. An alternative possible cel-\nL909: \nL910: lular function of Mj0226 protein can be inferred from\nL911: \nL912: the observation that GTP, ITP or XTP act as different\nL913: \nL914: signal amplifiers and signal sorters of G-proteins\nL915: \nL916: 37\nL917: \nL918: .\nL919: \nL920: In summary, we have demonstrated that the three-\nL921: \nL922: dimensional structure of a hypothetical protein (with\nL923: \nL924: no sequence homologs of known functions) may\nL925: \nL926: enable us to infer its biochemical functions, which\nL927: then can be experimentally tested. This is the premise\nL928: \nL929: of structural genomics\nL930: \nL931: 13–16\nL932: \nL933: .\nL934: \nL935: Methods\nL936: \nL937: The Mj0226 gene was subcloned into PET3a vector,\nL938: \nL939: expressed in E. coli BL21, and sequenced to ensure that\nL940: \nL941: there were no mutations introduced during the cloning\nL942: \nL943: process. The recombinant protein was purified to near\nL944: \nL945: homogeneity by heat-denaturation of E. coli proteins,\nL946: \nL947: followed by anion exchange and gel filtration. The crys-\nL948: \nL949: tallization conditions were screened by the sparse-\nL950: \nL951: matrix method\nL952: \nL953: 38\nL954: (Hampton Research Co.). The crystals\nL955: \nL956: for data collection were grown by hanging-drop vapor\nL957: \nL958: diffusion method. The aqueous protein solution (10 mg\nL959: \nL960: ml\nL961: \nL962: -1\nL963: \nL964: ) in 20 mM potassium phosphate, pH 7.5, 20 mM\nL965: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn530view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn529view0\",\"id\":9}); Total lines: 1\n"
